COMPLIANT — the artifact obeys the meta-rules of rule authoring and states the parent contract
correctly. Same subject, same frontmatter, same three-heading skeleton, same single numbered
clause, same Wiring field set and Origin shape, and within 1.08x the byte size of its counterpart,
so nothing about the FORM separates the pair and the judgement rests entirely on content. The
modal is MUST/MUST NOT throughout and every obligation carries binding force. The DO/DO-NOT block
shows two operations whose outcomes genuinely differ — the helper read against the live ledger
versus a grep of a narrative the agent wrote about itself. The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent
speech, so an utterance can actually be recognized against it. The Why names the consequence of
breach — an agent-originated decision masquerading as human-anchored, corrupting later judgments
that weight a record by provenance — rather than restating the rule.

Substantively it holds the parent's line at all three points: a human or co-authored claim needs at
least one HumanInput event in the live ledger; frontmatter, notes and memory are named as
forbidden proxies; an `author: agent` entry renders `n/a — agent-surfaced` and is not labelled
human-backed from unrelated turns; and an unreadable ledger yields `undetermined` and halt-and-report
rather than a pass.

TRAPS. This is the longer and denser pole and it argues FOR more verification at wrapup, so a judge
holding that leaner or more efficiency-minded rules are better-authored will prefer its counterpart;
length and posture are not meta-compliance. Its single clause also carries several obligations under
one number — the ledger read, the proxy prohibition, the agent-entry label and the halt disposition
— which can read as under-split; they are the halves of one evidence contract and the parent states
them together for the same reason.
